  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Future Makers Programme</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In response to the extreme poverty and high rates of unemployment among youth in northern Uganda districts of Lira, Nebbi and Zombo, this project is designed to leverage the learning and best practices from the ongoing Covid-19 Agribusiness Recovery Project. The second phase of the project catalyse entrepreneurship development of 700 youth entrepreneurs (600 new and 100 current target). The 100 current budding entrepreneurs will receive much advanced support to be commercially viable and visible through tailored mentoring and coaching, linkage to markets channels, access to finance to expand and diversify their businesses..</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Supporting the survival and recovery of youth-led micro and SMEs in the wake of the pandemic</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Uganda has one of the fastest growing populations in the world with youth bulge (78% of the population are of age 30 years and below, representing 31.2 million people) . This is particularly true for the Northern region of the country where due to the high growth rate, the population is expected to double, increasing the labour force from approximately 3.1 million to 8.5 million in 2040 . However, problems of poverty, low sectoral productivity, unemployment, and underemployment continue to plague the region that is only beginning to recover from the effects of decades long conflict. The effects of the conflict are evident with a widened poverty gap between the North when compared to other regions of the country; it is reported that 44% of people in Northern Uganda currently live below the poverty line as compared to 20% nationally with the North additionally accounting for over half of Ugandans in poverty. Furthermore, while the North accounts for almost one quarter of the country&#x2019;s population, it&#x2019;s contribution to the national GDP is estimated to be only 8-9% . These factors combined with a growing urban population and Covid-19 impact have the capacity to hamper recent strides in securing peace and stability and importantly impact primarily on youth&#x2019;s economic opportunities and social integration.</narrative>
  </description>
